Abstract

This document defines the canonical structure of every Cognitive Element within the Memory Operating System (MOS).

It establishes the semantic model governing Cognitive Elements, Cognitive Facets, Cognitive Relationships, Cognitive Operations, Cognitive Transactions, Cognitive Events, Version Evolution, and the MOS Semantic Layer.

This specification intentionally defines semantics rather than implementation.

Any storage engine, graph database, API, programming language, or runtime MAY implement these semantics differently, provided the observable behavior remains identical.

1. Purpose

RFC-0001 defines what cognition is.

RFC-0002 defines how cognition evolves.

RFC-0003 defines what cognition is composed of.

It specifies the universal structure shared by every Cognitive Element in the Cognitive State Space.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Cognitive Elements
Cognitive Facets
Cognitive Relationships
Cognitive Operations
Cognitive Transactions
Cognitive Events
Version Evolution
Semantic Composition
Semantic Layer

This specification does not define:

Memory domains
Storage schemas
SQL
Graph databases
Serialization
APIs
Network protocols
Worker implementations
3. Composition Axiom

MOS defines one universal rule of composition.

Every Cognitive Element SHALL consist of a stable Cognitive Identity composed with one or more Cognitive Facets, modified exclusively through Cognitive Operations executed within Cognitive Transactions.

Every concept defined later derives from this axiom.

