5. Cognitive Element

A Cognitive Element is the universal semantic primitive of MOS.

Every persistent cognitive artifact SHALL be represented by exactly one Cognitive Element.

No persistent cognition SHALL exist outside a Cognitive Element.

Every Cognitive Element SHALL possess:

Identity
Semantic meaning
Lifecycle
Version history
Provenance
Relationships
Operations

The internal representation is implementation-defined.

6. Cognitive Identity

Identity represents conceptual continuity rather than storage identity.

A Cognitive Identity SHALL remain stable throughout the lifetime of the represented concept.

Identity SHALL survive:

Version creation
Storage migration
Graph projection
Replication
Synchronization
Serialization

Identity SHALL NOT be reassigned.

Implementation identifiers (UUIDs, hashes, etc.) are not part of the semantic model.

7. Cognitive Facets

A Cognitive Facet represents one independent semantic dimension of a Cognitive Element.

Facets are compositional.

Facets SHALL be orthogonal.

A facet SHALL describe one concern only.

A facet SHALL NOT modify another facet directly.

A facet MAY evolve independently.

Future specifications define canonical facet types.

8. Cognitive Relationships

Relationships are Cognitive Elements.

Relationships therefore possess:

Identity
Facets
Version history
Provenance
Operations
Events

Relationships MAY connect:

Elements
Relationships
Transactions
Events

MOS therefore supports higher-order cognition.

9. Cognitive Operations

A Cognitive Operation represents one atomic semantic transformation.

Operations SHALL be deterministic with respect to their declared inputs.

Operations SHALL preserve:

Identity
Provenance
Version history
Constraints
Policies

Operations SHALL NOT directly modify storage.

Operations modify semantic state.

10. Cognitive Transactions

A Cognitive Transaction groups one or more Cognitive Operations into one atomic cognitive change.

A transaction SHALL either:

Complete successfully, or
Produce no semantic change.

Transactions SHALL preserve consistency of the Cognitive State Space.

Transactions SHALL expose:

Identity
Timestamp
Actor
Provenance
Operation set
Resulting events

The implementation of transactional guarantees is implementation-defined.

11. Cognitive Events

Every successful Cognitive Operation SHALL generate one or more Cognitive Events.

Events represent immutable historical facts describing cognitive evolution.

Events SHALL NEVER be modified.

Events SHALL NEVER be deleted.

Events SHALL remain permanently reconstructable.

Event sourcing MAY be used.

Event semantics SHALL be preserved.

12. Version Evolution

MOS never overwrites cognition.

Instead:

State V1

↓

Transaction

↓

Operations

↓

Events

↓

State V2

Version evolution SHALL preserve:

Identity
Provenance
Historical reconstruction
Explainability

Alternative version branches MAY exist.

13. Semantic Composition

Every Cognitive Element SHALL satisfy the following composition rules.

Exactly one identity.

One or more facets.

Zero or more relationships.

Zero or more operations.

Zero or more events.

Unlimited versions.

Unlimited projections.

Semantic meaning SHALL emerge from composition rather than inheritance.

14. Semantic Consistency

Every compliant implementation SHALL preserve:

Identity consistency.

Relationship consistency.

Version consistency.

Transaction consistency.

Provenance consistency.

Policy consistency.

Constraint consistency.

Semantic consistency SHALL be independent of storage implementation.

15. Explainability

Every Cognitive Element SHALL be explainable.

An implementation SHALL reconstruct:

Origin
Supporting observations
Producing operations
Producing transaction
Version lineage
Relationships
Policies
Constraints

Explainability SHALL NOT require regenerating reasoning using an external model.

16. Extensibility

MOS is extensible by construction.

Implementations MAY define:

New Facets
New Operations
New Relationship Types
New Policies
New Constraints

Extensions SHALL preserve semantic compatibility.

Existing semantics SHALL NOT be redefined.

17. Compliance

An implementation MAY claim:

MOS Core v0.1 – RFC-0003 Compliant

only if every normative requirement contained within this specification is satisfied.
