Appendix B — The Cognitive Mutation Model

One of the biggest refinements in this RFC is introducing Cognitive Transactions as the canonical unit of change.

Every modification to the Cognitive State Space follows the same semantic lifecycle:

External Stimulus
        │
        ▼
 Cognitive Force
        │
        ▼
Cognitive Transaction
        │
        ▼
Cognitive Operations
        │
        ▼
 Cognitive Events
        │
        ▼
 Version Evolution
        │
        ▼
 Updated Cognitive State

This model gives MOS a clean separation of concerns:

Forces express why cognition changes.
Transactions define the atomic unit of change.
Operations define what changes.
Events record what happened.
Versions preserve history.
State Space represents current cognition.
The next refinement: RFC-0004

After completing RFC-0003, I think the core kernel is now essentially complete. The next RFC should deliberately avoid talking about databases or storage and instead answer a different question:

How is cognition organized?

Rather than introducing "episodic memory," "semantic memory," "working memory," and similar concepts as separate storage systems, RFC-0004 should define them as Cognitive Domains—logical projections over the same Cognitive State Space, each with its own activation rules, lifecycle, and behavioral semantics.

That keeps the architecture beautifully layered:

RFC-0001 — What cognition is (Core Principles)
RFC-0002 — How cognition changes (Cognitive Dynamics)
RFC-0003 — What cognition is composed of (Element Model)
RFC-0004 — How cognition is organized (Cognitive Domains)

From there, the Storage Spec and API Spec become straightforward mappings of these semantics into concrete implementations, without introducing any new cognitive concepts. I think that's a strong sign that the architecture is converging toward a stable and reusable foundation.
